#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133508 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b03ea89-5152-5ff4-813a-ca985d60ed5b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonality"}] :question/id #uuid "bbb751a9-ef0d-58cf-b788-18594dd67337" :question/math-academy-id "e-5560" :question/problem "For the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/b85705767814eae0f99345888b1f0c65a89dbbc76458d7093a02a20937d2e1c3.png) which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/85cd8d0ebcd70de93ec2bd5007907a02d81daadd27b348e7c72ccef53654d881.png)\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/e03fd2577b8d7067f4507d0ee6fe732d3786a494134cd967d3c65de01e28e709.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H$ and $V$ as $\\{\\mathbf{h}_1,\\mathbf{h}_2\\}$ and $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}$ respectively.  \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $\\mathbf{u}$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-6)+6\\cdot 1=0,\\,✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =3\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-3)+6\\cdot (-1)=-6\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf h_1$ but $\\mathbf u \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_2.$ Because $\\mathbf u$ is not orthogonal to every vector in $H,$ we have $\\mathbf{u}\\not \\perp H.$\n\n- For the set $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6})=0\\,✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =3\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 3+6\\cdot 0=0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf v_2.$ Because $\\mathbf u$ is orthogonal to every vector in $V,$ we have $\\mathbf{u}\\perp V.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133509 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9aa43961-5b6a-5c8b-adea-809d0a693cd1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of a Vector Orthogonal to a Set"}] :question/id #uuid "bbed6e9e-f63e-599f-ab8e-71cff46cfcfd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5561" :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/d648a1f36d090c680a6b72f30ff447ce78623f746157348f30a02fa4ea245c66.png) is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/d29f02d59f540cae1aae6057e05eeeb349d84de1ac9be11f6e3b131083f6905a.png) find the value of $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_1=0$ and $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_2=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/608fd40128068aa5f05e6b256081bd2e0ebbf569fca4c9bae4977740eb4db2ce.png)\n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_2=-x_3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=-3x_3.$ So, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/d6d7ea3c500d1112c64e19da288fbd7ad7e6ed912cf3121a2292427368b19f0e.png)\n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we get the vector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/c4875bc998184d934c9b43113cd31091b792f1c04ff4387d4b8ab8a7a08f90d4.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1.$\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}=\\dfrac{-3+1}{-1}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133510 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b2f7e06-54f8-5a9b-a926-362fb087b59c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Complement of a Span"}] :question/id #uuid "ef531549-f5be-5002-b94f-1c7ee8682784" :question/math-academy-id "e-5562" :question/problem "Find $H^{\\perp}$ if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/889411800149371acab53c44829a050d69df697ce6723dd95b9045ddf873e484.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2.$ \n\nA vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/ed0e0a3a78ff25be588f62627ffd5bae4074f6c59b8e88ad160e4407253ae6dd.png) belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_1=0$ and $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_2=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/af507041d4aa3cc7dfb415afc672b5a7875f9f1a27acced81505797e5f92d7e0.png) \n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation we get $x_2=-\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.$ Substituting this into first equation, we obtain $x_1 = -\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.$\n\nSo, the general solution to the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/34b29c1902a7c441b2ac15be91ffb60b8856ab7dc2706146d09eb02f081827ba.png)\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/496154a4d67c0b7861da038306f1d5cc65188cc036cf8514b24603c7ce4cd948.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133555 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb11d128-ee1b-5c35-aefb-1455570d20c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Which Vectors Belong to the Orthogonal Complement of a Set"}] :question/id #uuid "b7cb0b20-e2a6-56d7-b568-ae08c259e581" :question/math-academy-id "e-5913" :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/bee23fc9beaddffe7fd60b2c2bbd2d2ee952a277960c5d08fcad5b934e209f64.png) Which of the following vectors belong to $H^{\\perp}?$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/abfbcbc8c3b942b93faf4b48336daa54345b97fb21d3aa11685f0d29f206590e.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2.$   \n\nNow, a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if $\\mathbf{v}$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products $\\mathbf{v}_i \\cdot \\mathbf{h}_j$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_1$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 3=1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf v_1 \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_1,$ which means $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\notin H^{\\perp}.$ We do not need to test $\\mathbf v_1\\cdot \\mathbf h_2.$\n\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_2$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-2)+4\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 3=4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf v_2 \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_1,$ which means $\\mathbf{v}_2 \\notin H^{\\perp}.$ We do not need to test $\\mathbf v_2\\cdot \\mathbf h_2.$\n\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_3$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =5\\cdot (-2)+8\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot 3=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =5\\cdot 2+8\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf v_3 \\perp \\mathbf h_1$ and $\\mathbf v_3 \\perp \\mathbf h_2,$ which means $\\mathbf{v}_3 \\in H^{\\perp}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{v}_3$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb11d128-ee1b-5c35-aefb-1455570d20c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Which Vectors Belong to the Orthogonal Complement of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb256e3-5266-515b-8c23-0b55d959f13c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352489} {:answer/id #uuid "e5374f5c-abd5-55b9-be3f-308ab596bf8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352490} {:answer/id #uuid "bb8b970b-3155-52fc-8275-44c8a13f54e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352491} {:answer/id #uuid "6967de3e-fda0-5e12-80fa-6328217d2af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352492} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae7405b-f923-5d5f-8c5c-b51f31ee7fc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eb256e3-5266-515b-8c23-0b55d959f13c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352489} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c6bbc6f-3090-583b-a850-44b490470a49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2eb17a02-3ec7-5adc-ace9-ee9207acfe63" :question/math-academy-id "q-140468" :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =5\\cdot (-6)+(-2)\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 5=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =5\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 4+6\\cdot (-11)=-64\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =5\\cdot (-6)+(-14)\\cdot 0+(-6)\\cdot 5=-60\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =5\\cdot (-6)+14\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 5=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =5\\cdot 2+14\\cdot 4+6\\cdot (-11)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{3}$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b03ea89-5152-5ff4-813a-ca985d60ed5b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e14510f7-eb82-52c6-857b-a2605ba357fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352495} {:answer/id #uuid "3f92a11e-54fc-5b42-bcf6-1a73f9c321ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352496} {:answer/id #uuid "93080b4a-2df0-5dda-aba9-bd569b930854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352497} {:answer/id #uuid "6979df5d-7cf4-59a9-bb45-c41ba69584d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352498} {:answer/id #uuid "10ca7dd0-7c76-5dcb-8c53-0cee5aabea9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f92a11e-54fc-5b42-bcf6-1a73f9c321ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352496} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cded761-86e2-5cb5-89dd-3df4c9901354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c796dfc-19c9-5431-bc95-d9fa1ccc2efa" :question/math-academy-id "q-39623" :question/problem "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $V={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as ${{h}_{1},{h}_{2}},{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and ${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =1\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{3} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot (-1)=-2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $V.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 4+1\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =1\\cdot (2)+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1=2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b2f7e06-54f8-5a9b-a926-362fb087b59c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Complement of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ced44c5f-6c71-5bfa-bfe8-83798798d8a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352502} {:answer/id #uuid "019fea90-0b41-5eae-9565-d16e1004ef30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352503} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c84d62-9012-5a2a-90f8-309d5fa0fa3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352504} {:answer/id #uuid "ee94cb2c-2e03-5847-a83c-60b3f2cee888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352505} {:answer/id #uuid "97cc8452-36c3-5c5d-8a31-9c0abb869fad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97cc8452-36c3-5c5d-8a31-9c0abb869fad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352506} :answer-field/id #uuid "98004a82-5a17-50b1-8ac6-40115640f425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5aabf0b-4546-5bc0-a1a8-38812d835d5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-39625" :question/problem "Find ${H}^{⊥}$, if $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n\n$$\n{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b2f7e06-54f8-5a9b-a926-362fb087b59c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Complement of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a55111ab-339f-58c4-9f17-45bf9ef7ca5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352509} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f97b58-7bd4-5736-b682-c2906b5dc9b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352510} {:answer/id #uuid "18f0a33b-8a03-5992-9fcf-75ecfee9ccbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352511} {:answer/id #uuid "4aae0962-46b5-5b00-8c68-ae1860310fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352512} {:answer/id #uuid "8cac39d6-5775-55a1-b9a6-27e278a0d412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4aae0962-46b5-5b00-8c68-ae1860310fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352512} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f82fa42-5949-59e4-909a-c58936705442" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43c590fa-d89b-58cd-94e7-3ff278816239" :question/math-academy-id "q-39626" :question/problem "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$ if  $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the  second equation we get ${x}_{2}=0$ and then from the first equation we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.$ So, any vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b03ea89-5152-5ff4-813a-ca985d60ed5b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89d40cdb-97d6-52bf-a447-af5b54a22525" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131367} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f45eea-b3b4-568b-8d08-3ebf845c33cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131368} {:answer/id #uuid "c7981d7f-c4c4-5179-9dbb-4457ff8cb3b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131369} {:answer/id #uuid "e5842910-3cbd-5879-a73d-015215d3f586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131370} {:answer/id #uuid "23a0a656-e613-55d7-a396-26af793292d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5f45eea-b3b4-568b-8d08-3ebf845c33cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131368} :answer-field/id #uuid "0842f212-ed40-522a-9d77-4d2d5c10f9ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2175cca2-c03f-5359-9970-f5c376ea77b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39630" :question/problem "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $u⊥H,\\,$ where $H={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u⊥V,\\,$ where $V={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u⊥W,\\,$ where $W={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as ${{h}_{1},{h}_{2}},{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and ${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-1)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =(-1)\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 3=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =(-1)\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =(-1)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-4)+(-1)\\cdot (-2)=-3\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸V.$ Notice that we stopped after finding the first non-orthogonal vector in the set.\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =(-1)\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot 8=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =(-1)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 2=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb11d128-ee1b-5c35-aefb-1455570d20c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Which Vectors Belong to the Orthogonal Complement of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6b3091c-1462-5a08-88f4-c47bf70f85e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{1}$ and $v_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131373} {:answer/id #uuid "e7cc2b80-2702-5c1a-bb82-01c19fc791dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131374} {:answer/id #uuid "f3cd9495-30b8-5068-a336-709d11e5471d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131375} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea5d17f-928a-5738-8f75-44a0a51f0662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{2}$ and $v_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131376} {:answer/id #uuid "78b8322b-069d-53e8-b8b4-943f90172823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6b3091c-1462-5a08-88f4-c47bf70f85e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{1}$ and $v_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131373} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e925a7b-21c1-508c-a6b0-151470cf3901" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9b7b645-faaf-50a2-b81a-1590fa3480cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-39631" :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =1\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 3=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot (-2)=-6\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot (-2)+(-6)\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =3\\cdot 3+(-6)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 3=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b2f7e06-54f8-5a9b-a926-362fb087b59c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Complement of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "808ddb04-a02f-5704-985c-45666cd27ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352516} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d7394f-767f-551a-8c8b-7c67a3e2200a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352517} {:answer/id #uuid "76a9f39b-0de5-59a8-b567-aa1c62d49d37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352518} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1ad34d-f427-5d79-b170-5854331b0b4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352519} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8541b3-a914-5227-9fc1-234d1ac8ba0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "808ddb04-a02f-5704-985c-45666cd27ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352516} :answer-field/id #uuid "328371b9-0dee-5c7b-9f07-147871d90280" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e102f80-1de4-5da6-83a4-86ff00dee1d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-39633" :question/problem "Find  ${H}^{⊥}$, if $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0$  and from the first one, we obtain ${x}_{1}=0.$ So, the solution to the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n\n$$\n{H}^{⊥}={0}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b2f7e06-54f8-5a9b-a926-362fb087b59c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Complement of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df0d5c43-4e78-5eb4-acb3-368c613fb166" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352522} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf95c6c-44d4-55d5-ba50-c0e43fb23cfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352523} {:answer/id #uuid "9400bdbe-9b15-5401-8bf6-8024f2cfcb78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352524} {:answer/id #uuid "530bb423-e122-51ea-b815-c6a6c827e045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352525} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff7581a-c70f-5adc-9045-ca576e7c0412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9400bdbe-9b15-5401-8bf6-8024f2cfcb78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352524} :answer-field/id #uuid "de2ea042-b21f-5ab0-a80f-c85930528821" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17bd69d7-65e4-535e-bfc3-813ca9368a10" :question/math-academy-id "q-39915" :question/problem "Find ${H}^{⊥}$ if $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n\n$$\n{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b2f7e06-54f8-5a9b-a926-362fb087b59c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Complement of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8d56431-6bc6-5392-8f22-95bef4f62458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352529} {:answer/id #uuid "343d271b-13d8-5e6a-b813-b7759f7c6979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352530} {:answer/id #uuid "4060b23f-b621-566f-aedb-bbf5c214f225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -3{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352531} {:answer/id #uuid "37262b32-3266-5efd-b22c-3e782f553a7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352532} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac1ee39-fcdc-5877-ab99-2a27ca4e7fd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8d56431-6bc6-5392-8f22-95bef4f62458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{H}^{⊥}={0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352529} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a850056-2423-5e1c-a70e-e59f359677f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bafd46f-4d0d-5847-af92-1b47a9fb2b93" :question/math-academy-id "q-39916" :question/problem "Find ${H}^{⊥}$, if $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {x}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0$  and from the first one, we obtain ${x}_{1}=0.$ So, the solution to the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n\n$$\n{H}^{⊥}={0}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9aa43961-5b6a-5c8b-adea-809d0a693cd1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of a Vector Orthogonal to a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "842b5c5c-cc5c-50d2-b823-6def6fca04e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132957} {:answer/id #uuid "da8821b3-5d06-5e21-80f5-a553896d64c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132958} {:answer/id #uuid "15e8b357-5098-57b9-8c74-02a2282b9714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132959} {:answer/id #uuid "38f6361e-a902-560f-9217-4c195d638701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132960} {:answer/id #uuid "660803b9-f7f4-568d-8a7c-5d45e4320d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15e8b357-5098-57b9-8c74-02a2282b9714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132959} :answer-field/id #uuid "142bdc42-c623-5b18-9590-d0d791655eb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e055065d-3cdf-5f06-8b7f-a22a1c681c38" :question/math-academy-id "q-71102" :question/problem "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{c}{a+b}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=1,$ and $c=2.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{c}{a+b}=\\frac{2}{1+1}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b03ea89-5152-5ff4-813a-ca985d60ed5b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea0a9a47-2dd0-5339-ae25-9816af2363db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352536} {:answer/id #uuid "ed82545d-2a4f-537a-8095-616f56c8565a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352537} {:answer/id #uuid "01575bd0-ec74-5d36-bf32-d99ac07c2904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352538} {:answer/id #uuid "0936b5e9-a41a-5dc1-ad92-f7a55ee2bf69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352539} {:answer/id #uuid "6b1926d7-a020-5cbc-be3c-cf1c86cca801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01575bd0-ec74-5d36-bf32-d99ac07c2904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352538} :answer-field/id #uuid "74b3bdf6-4a8f-50ce-8a92-4a38c8247624" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43395a07-7c5c-5ed1-938a-d890d23531a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-80500" :question/problem "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $V={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as ${{h}_{1},{h}_{2}},{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and ${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0=-2 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is not orthogonal to the set $H.$ Notice that we stopped after finding the first non-orthogonal vector in the set.\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (-1)=1\\ne 0 &  & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, $u$ is not orthogonal to the set $H.$ Notice that, again,  we stopped after finding the first non-orthogonal vector in the set.\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b03ea89-5152-5ff4-813a-ca985d60ed5b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d81c3d3-21c9-5426-a430-3b4ee2454892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352543} {:answer/id #uuid "91416fb9-c106-57d3-bc8e-6b7031924fd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352544} {:answer/id #uuid "c462ddfc-dacc-5a0f-b219-a9d23b987ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352545} {:answer/id #uuid "d46cfe09-6563-588d-b549-8ca5cc9a7673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352546} {:answer/id #uuid "c73d1929-a25f-50f3-a3c0-4b1573519a38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d46cfe09-6563-588d-b549-8ca5cc9a7673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352546} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f10d5a3-1e06-58d3-b2de-b7ecad475aaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4e9d2c3-0185-539c-af7c-b2fafdc3f5af" :question/math-academy-id "q-80501" :question/problem "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $u⊥H,\\,$ where $H={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u⊥V,\\,$ where $V={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u⊥W,\\,$ where $W={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as ${{h}_{1},{h}_{2}},{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ and ${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 2=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 1=-5\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot 1=2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸V.$ Notice that we stopped after finding the first non-orthogonal vector in the set.\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot 1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b03ea89-5152-5ff4-813a-ca985d60ed5b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "640ebf3c-6637-507d-857d-d581fc94bdb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352550} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccbdbc7-e03c-52f2-b162-09b518d95a38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352551} {:answer/id #uuid "334e5ee6-f86b-5758-bf87-93c56cb4334f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352552} {:answer/id #uuid "ce942a27-1399-5d7e-b428-585e564789dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352553} {:answer/id #uuid "87f51194-9db1-50e9-bbc9-39044a43f290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87f51194-9db1-50e9-bbc9-39044a43f290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352554} :answer-field/id #uuid "abe3cf4d-40aa-5c2b-9b5a-1911f9a3e716" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fcfdcaf-2d9f-5535-a1fc-a07761b92dec" :question/math-academy-id "q-80504" :question/problem "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $V={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $V,H$- and $W$ as ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},{{h}_{1},{h}_{2}}$ and ${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =3\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)=0\\,✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3=5\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is not orthogonal to the set $V.$ Notice that we stopped after finding the first non-orthogonal vector in the set.\n\n- For the set $H:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)=0,\\,✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =3\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1=0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =3\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 4=4\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is not orthogonal to the set $W.$ Notice that we stopped after finding the first non-orthogonal vector in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9aa43961-5b6a-5c8b-adea-809d0a693cd1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of a Vector Orthogonal to a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af29897a-31e1-5f99-af24-cb6c66a29c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352557} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c5f930-c6b2-524f-a358-d2648cf36ea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352558} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e4a51f-4e25-5105-8e30-2df87efc371d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352559} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9d52fd-ebef-5612-942a-a962b5ad6420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352560} {:answer/id #uuid "f2fa4fe6-5c66-58c1-8f24-cc05c05dd476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5e4a51f-4e25-5105-8e30-2df87efc371d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352559} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad77c456-60b3-5ec0-ab5c-f052fc9fd602" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b90f9f7-06ed-50e2-83d2-b6c61501a094" :question/math-academy-id "q-80532" :question/problem "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{a+c}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-10{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -10 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -10 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=5,$ $b=4,$ and $c=3.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a+c}{b}=\\frac{5+3}{4}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9aa43961-5b6a-5c8b-adea-809d0a693cd1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of a Vector Orthogonal to a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c83fe86e-bcf8-5333-aba0-15d8f645e4cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352564} {:answer/id #uuid "1f793c13-3638-5f12-b386-01fafec9e4dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352565} {:answer/id #uuid "8211f2a2-1254-5244-91c5-7e54eab3716f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352566} {:answer/id #uuid "a603744a-7b57-5993-a204-c242a3fbbb0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352567} {:answer/id #uuid "88547203-5295-5962-ab6d-607592d7cfbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c83fe86e-bcf8-5333-aba0-15d8f645e4cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352564} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1bde0ca-be00-595a-a128-fbe6fe79d17d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "996c67e0-4eb8-54bd-95e8-5e926007963e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80535" :question/problem "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{c}{a+b}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-5,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=2.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{c}{a+b}=\\frac{2}{-5+(-1)}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9aa43961-5b6a-5c8b-adea-809d0a693cd1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of a Vector Orthogonal to a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d150da8-c504-564b-8b2f-ad3dab9bb511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352571} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6e6318-40a7-5104-89ce-0af44ae24cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352572} {:answer/id #uuid "0f22b530-cacf-56f1-8e89-1eda726833be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352573} {:answer/id #uuid "c0994f64-b0f6-5baf-a085-5d9807301433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352574} {:answer/id #uuid "1f569f2a-0a1a-55de-9794-f9dd192504e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f569f2a-0a1a-55de-9794-f9dd192504e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352575} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7e967f6-2fd9-5176-9965-3863c275266f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4da05a37-ec63-5545-abce-ccdafebfd7db" :question/math-academy-id "q-80537" :question/problem "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{a+b}{c}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1$, we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=1.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a+b}{c}=\\frac{1+(-2)}{1}=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9aa43961-5b6a-5c8b-adea-809d0a693cd1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of a Vector Orthogonal to a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3541c31c-791e-5cc1-8ac8-f3b3a7128207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352578} {:answer/id #uuid "043fb885-5c82-5d19-92cd-07c5efbb2f9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352579} {:answer/id #uuid "8470d6c4-ca04-5f3d-a129-08ffe79874f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352580} {:answer/id #uuid "f2adab80-4b2f-5869-a6eb-541ee8300a32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352581} {:answer/id #uuid "9742de96-45eb-5ed3-b079-484fe0609ee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "043fb885-5c82-5d19-92cd-07c5efbb2f9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352579} :answer-field/id #uuid "d231cfbe-b195-5918-8b28-5eb5aa88cc19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2f638a3-52dd-5aeb-8f41-e7b02ea6bb20" :question/math-academy-id "q-80538" :question/problem "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a+c}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=4$, we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-10,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=4.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a+c}=\\frac{-3}{-10+4}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb11d128-ee1b-5c35-aefb-1455570d20c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Which Vectors Belong to the Orthogonal Complement of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7568f9-ca77-50be-bccb-1ddc0145f3a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352585} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac30d9f-65c4-54cd-b89b-1162d23a396f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352586} {:answer/id #uuid "d66ae6b7-cace-581f-a405-92eaa867ce59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352587} {:answer/id #uuid "e73f0e06-a224-5143-bcbf-ab5d12ecd20d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352588} {:answer/id #uuid "ae50014b-6d06-5aaa-9d3c-1928e1eb3e8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f7568f9-ca77-50be-bccb-1ddc0145f3a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352585} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d3ba89b-f40c-5e5b-98d3-0fd087424214" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2706fd95-4749-5416-af7f-b2e6a7351f4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80576" :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let  $h=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ A vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to $h.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot h$ for $i=1,2,3.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot h & =1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 3=10\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot h & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 3=-4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot h & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 3=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{3}$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb11d128-ee1b-5c35-aefb-1455570d20c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Which Vectors Belong to the Orthogonal Complement of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73495f42-005e-59ec-8c24-18ab2fd69134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352592} {:answer/id #uuid "1d953562-9b7e-5b35-a92b-af95d99ebf48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352593} {:answer/id #uuid "f717704c-792a-5945-a2e0-bad27270fd5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352594} {:answer/id #uuid "3637d8af-da86-54c5-a0ea-0c409a667d4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352595} {:answer/id #uuid "483a8d0e-b91f-57af-9c93-caa2ce6a7a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73495f42-005e-59ec-8c24-18ab2fd69134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352592} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1fa6ff6-7b02-50a2-be10-2383b8018503" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e741dfd-bd80-514a-860d-823db3cd516a" :question/math-academy-id "q-80581" :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let  $h=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ A vector  $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to $h.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot h$ for $i=1,2,3.$   \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot h & =4\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot (-4)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, ${v}_{1}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot h & =2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 4+(-3)\\cdot (-4)=30\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot h & =1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 4+4\\cdot (-4)=-19\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{1}$  only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb11d128-ee1b-5c35-aefb-1455570d20c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Which Vectors Belong to the Orthogonal Complement of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4877dd42-1cb2-5b26-8a82-c79864c32c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352599} {:answer/id #uuid "7c27e091-9c1a-52ad-837d-3f88b9fcedca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352600} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4261c9-14bb-57ad-a051-e2f96f105354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352601} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0731da-2baa-5423-8272-fa09ec0c509b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352602} {:answer/id #uuid "095b7500-f88e-5c6a-81a6-1f744df796ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4877dd42-1cb2-5b26-8a82-c79864c32c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352599} :answer-field/id #uuid "152aaa61-f97a-50f9-a7ff-79d595302c48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63cd77a9-8a5f-5279-a45d-65255e657187" :question/math-academy-id "q-80584" :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$ \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$  \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$ \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 4=19\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 4=8\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =7\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 4=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =7\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{3}$ only.\""}]}
